==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|ih}}
* {{l|ang|iċċ}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|[[*ek]], [[*ik]]}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*éǵh₂}}. Germanic cognates include {{cog|ofs|ik}}, {{cog|osx|ik}}, {{cog|odt|ik}} ({{cog|nl|ik}}), {{cog|goh|ih}} ({{cog|de|ich}}), {{cog|non|ek}} ({{cog|sv|jag}}), {{cog|got|𐌹𐌺}}. The Indo-European root, in various forms, is also the source of {{cog|sa|अहम्|tr=ahám}}, {{cog|la|egō}} ({{cog|fr|je}}, {{cog|es|yo}}, {{cog|it|io}} etc.), {{cog|grc|ἐγώ}}, {{cog|lt|aš}}, {{cog|lv|es}}, {{cog|ae|𐬀𐬰𐬆𐬨}}, {{cog|cu|азъ}} ({{cog|ru|я}}), {{cog|xcl|ես}}. For declined derivations, see under {{m|ang|mē}}, {{m|ang|wē}} etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/itʃ/|/ik/|lang=ang}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|cat2=personal pronouns|head2=iċ|head=ic}}

# [[I]], used by the speaker referring to themselves as the subject, or in agreement with that subject

====Declension====
{{ang-pronoun-pers|{{l|ang|iċ}}, {{l|ang|ih}}|{{l|ang|wit}}|{{l|ang|we|wē}}|{{l|ang|mec|meċ}}, {{l|ang|me|mē}}|{{l|ang|uncit}}, {{l|ang|unc}}|{{l|ang|usic|ūsiċ}}, {{l|ang|us|ūs}}|{{l|ang|min|mīn}}|{{l|ang|uncer}}|{{l|ang|user|ūser}}, {{l|ang|ure|ūre}}|{{l|ang|me|mē}}|{{l|ang|unc}}|{{l|ang|us|ūs}}}}

====Descendants====
* Middle English: {{l|enm|ich}}, {{l|enm|ik}}, {{l|enm|I}}
** English: {{l|en|ich}}, {{l|en|I}}
** Scots: {{l|sco|I}}, {{l|sco|ik}}

